// case: hex-escape-zero-is-never-decoded
// what: `print("\x00")` printed the four characters `\x00`.
//
// The byte escape is documented as `\xHH` with two hex digits, so `\x00` is
// the NUL byte and the four characters are wrong. The builder's decoder had
// `hi * 16 + lo > 0`, which rejects a byte whose value is zero -- and the only
// hex escape that names such a byte is `\x00`. So the one case the rule
// promised, it did the opposite of.
//
// It is now a compile error, and the reason is the interesting part. Decoding
// it would be worse than leaving it literal: an `orbit_string` is a
// NUL-terminated `const char*`, so a value containing a zero byte is truncated
// at that byte on the way into C. `escapeString` does not emit one, and it
// should not. An Orbit string cannot hold a NUL, and the language now says so
// at the point where the author can see why, instead of handing back four
// characters that look like an escape.

fn main() -> int {
    print("before\x00after")
    return 0
}
// expect-error: a string cannot contain a NUL byte
